Not entirely.  According to MLA guidelines, an ellipsis at the end of a
sentence should be preceded by a space and followed by a space and the
period.  Like so ... .  Others like APA and Chicago are likely different
in the minutiae.  For example, I'm fairly sure that one of them
recommends spaces around each period . . . but that feels awkward to me.
Another advises spaces around each period unless at the end of a
sentence, where the first period should have no space preceeding, as
here. . . .  

Of course, "trailing off" at the end of a sentence isn't a proper formal
usage anyway, so things are a bit ad hoc, but I'm not aware of any
American guidelines not recommending at least _some_ spacing.

